e sys package implementation task with platform-specific syscall abstractions for Darwin and Linux, including the public API surface and required documentation references.\n\nNow I'm looking at the specific requirements: implementing terminal dimension queries via ioctl, abstracting stat struct differences between platforms, handling process group operations, and setting up signal handlers for SIGPIPE, SIGHUP, and SIGWINCH.\n\nFor the design, I'm using the x/sys/unix package as the only external dependency, with platform-specific files selected via build tags while keeping the public API clean and platform-agnostic. The key constraint is that cmd/ packages can't import syscalls directly—they have to go through pkg/sys. I'm also abstracting away the differences in how Darwin and Linux represent file modification times and block counts so callers see a consistent interface.\n\nNow I need to verify this works: the package should compile on both Darwin and Linux, TermSize should report actual terminal dimensions, Stat and Lstat should return valid metadata on both platforms, and the process-related functions should at least compile successfully. I'm also checking that there are no vet warnings and that no code accidentally imports from x/sys directly.\n\nI'm reconsidering the requirement count—I have four main requirements here, which fits within the constraint of at most four PRD sub-requirements per task. These map directly to the top-level requirements in the PRD, so I'm good on that front.\n\nFor the title, I realize I don't actually know the PRD number yet, so using \"prd-sys R1-R4\" as a placeholder makes sense until the stitch agent can resolve it against the actual PRD file. The real issue is that I need to make sure the task description is self-contained enough for the agent to work with, even if I'm referencing a PRD file that might not exist at the path I'm assuming. I should probably let the agent handle finding and reading the correct PRD file, then use wha