t me finalize the YAML with the proper task definition and required reading references. Looking at the requirements for the DiffTest implementation, I need to define the struct fields including the normalization hooks for stdout and stderr, then implement the Run method that executes both binaries with identical inputs and compares their outputs against the expected values using those normalization functions if provided.\n\nThe test file needs to cover three scenarios: a passing case where all outputs match, a failing case where the exit code differs, and a case where the normalization hook strips trailing whitespace and the outputs still match after normalization.\n\nThe main source file should be named difftest.go following the naming convention specified in the planning constitution, with the test file as difftest_test.go.\n\nI'm using the standard library's exec package to run both binaries and capture their output streams separately, then extracting the exit code from the error when the command fails.\n\nI should use t.Errorf instead of t.Fatalf so all three potential mismatches get reported together rather than stopping at the first one.\n\nThe package can only depend on the Go standard library—testing, os/exec, bytes, os, and syscall for exit code handling—with no imports from other internal packages.\n\nNow I'm laying out the acceptance criteria: the build should succeed on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64, a passing test should complete without errors, a failing exit code should report both expected and actual values, and normalization functions like trimming trailing newlines should work correctly. I'm double-checking the YAML structure to make sure all the strings with colons are properly quoted. The text fields in the requirements contain colons, and since they're scalar values in YAML, I need to verify they're handled correctly—they should be fine as-is since they're not in flow context, but let me make sure the quoting is consistent throughout. Verifying all the text fields are properly quoted in the YAML structure, working through each requirement, design decision, and acceptance criterion to ensure colons and special characters are handled correctly. I'm finalizing the YAML structure now—the `note` fields are properly quoted where needed, and I've simplified AC4 to avoid escape sequence complications. The `files` section with its optional `note` subfield is appropriate for documenting the test file requirements, including the mandate that difftest_test.go contains at least three test cases.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19072,"cache_read_input_tokens":15967,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19072},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50113b3b-a4e1-4f80-8036-837075813d3d","uuid":"815de04a-a760-48c4-87bc-bce146e18c03"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01M7jZNLLNyvvh91XztcrE7E","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Step 1: Analysis\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal behavioral specifications. Man pages miss edge cases, flag interactions, and exit-code contracts. This project derives specs from C source, generates verified Go implementations via AI, and uses differential testing against Homebrew GNU binaries for parity.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify) with `cmd/` (utility implementations), `pkg/sys/` (syscall abstractions), `pkg/format/` (formatting), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), `magefiles/` (build targets, provided by cobbler-scaffold), and `docs/` (human-authored specs).\n\n**Current state**: Code exists only at generation-merged git tags (not on working branch). The working branch has specifications only. All four rel00.0 use cases are spec_complete, codestatus: not started. `magefiles/` is excluded from generation (DD7).\n\n**Current release**: rel00.0 — Foundational infrastructure. Four use cases:\n- rel00.0-uc001-magefiles: excluded from generation per DD7\n- rel00.0-uc002-format: spec_complete, not started\n- rel00.0-uc003-sys: spec_complete, not started\n- rel00.0-uc004-testutils: spec_complete, not started\n\n## Step 2: Priority reasoning\n\nWith one task allowed, the most foundational component is `pkg/testutils`. It is the acceptance mechanism for *every* utility — no differential test can run without it. While `pkg/format` and `pkg/sys` come before `pkg/testutils` alphabetically, `pkg/testutils` has uniquely higher functional priority: it unblocks verification for all subsequent code generation work, while `pkg/format` and `pkg/sys` are each needed by only a subset of utilities. Design decision DD2 explicitly identifies differential testing as the primary verification mechanism. `pkg/testutils` is also a leaf library (no internal dependencies), making it independently implementable.\n\nThe primary exported type is `DiffTest`; per planning constitution P7, the file must be `difftest.go`, not `testutils.go`.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement pkg/testutils DiffTest differential testing harness (rel00.0-uc004)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/testutils component section, design decision DD2 on differential testing as the primary verification mechanism, DD7 on generation scope exclusions)\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml (testutils PRD; if not found at this exact path, locate the testutils PRD under docs/specs/product-requirements/ using a glob on prd*-testutils.yaml)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/testutils/difftest.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"DiffTest struct and Run method -- primary exported type is DiffTest; file is named after the type, not the package (testutils.go is forbidden per naming convention)\"\n      - path: pkg/testutils/difftest_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Unit tests verifying DiffTest.Run behavior against a stub binary pair\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Define DiffTest struct with exported fields: Args []string (arguments for both binaries), Stdin []byte (content piped to stdin), Env []string (additional environment variables merged into the inherited environment), WantExit int (expected exit code), WantStdout []byte (expected stdout), WantStderr []byte (expected stderr), NormStdout func([]byte) []byte (optional hook applied to stdout before comparison), NormStderr func([]byte) []byte (optional hook applied to stderr before comparison).\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement func (tc DiffTest) Run(t *testing.T, refBin, goBin string) that executes both refBin and goBin with identical inputs: tc.Args as arguments, tc.Stdin piped to stdin, and an environment formed by merging os.Environ() with tc.Env.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"After executing both binaries, compare the Go binary exit code against WantExit, stdout against WantStdout, and stderr against WantStderr byte-for-byte (applying NormStdout and NormStderr hooks when non-nil before comparison); call t.Errorf for each mismatch, reporting the binary path, expected value, and actual value.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"difftest_test.go must exercise at least three cases: one where exit code, stdout, and stderr all match (t.Errorf not called); one where exit code mismatches (t.Errorf called with expected and actual exit codes); and one where a NormStdout hook strips trailing newlines causing an otherwise-mismatched stdout comparison to pass.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"The primary exported type is DiffTest; the main source file is difftest.go, not testutils.go. This naming rule is stated explicitly in the planning constitution: 'testutils/testutils.go is forbidden; if the primary type is DiffTest, the file is difftest.go. Test files mirror source: difftest_test.go'.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use os/exec.Command to launch each binary. Capture stdout and stderr independently with bytes.Buffer assigned to cmd.Stdout and cmd.Stderr. Extract the exit code from *exec.ExitError when cmd.Run returns a non-nil error.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Use t.Errorf (not t.Fatalf) so a single DiffTest.Run call reports all three possible mismatches -- exit code, stdout, stderr -- in one invocation without stopping at the first failure.\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"pkg/testutils must import only the Go standard library (testing, os/exec, bytes, os, syscall as needed). It must not import pkg/sys, pkg/format, or any cmd/ package. Only cmd/ test files import pkg/testutils.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"go build ./pkg/testutils/... succeeds with no compilation errors on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"A DiffTest where WantExit, WantStdout, and WantStderr all match the Go binary output completes Run without calling t.Errorf.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"A DiffTest where WantExit=0 but the Go binary exits with code 1 causes t.Errorf to be called with a message containing both the expected value (0) and the actual value (1).\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"A DiffTest with a NormStdout hook that strips trailing newlines passes when the Go binary stdout differs from WantStdout only in trailing newlines.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"go test ./pkg/testutils/... passes with all test cases green.\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"go vet ./pkg/testutils/... passes with no diagnostics.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19072,"cache_read_input_tokens":15967,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19072},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50113b3b-a4e1-4f80-8036-837075813d3d","uuid":"d0517863-e253-46d6-81fa-87e528fc81d6"}
